  "question": {
    "text": "According to law of equipartition of energy the molar specific heat of a diatomic gas at constant volume where the molecule has one additional vibrational mode is :-"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>9</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi>R</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>5</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi>R</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi>R</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>7</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi>R</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": true
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "D"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>A diatomic gas has 5 degrees of freedom apart from vibrational degree of freedom. For each additional vibrational mode, degree of freedom is increased by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math>. So new degree of freedom,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></math></p><p>Therefore, we require&nbsp;molar specific heat capacity at constant volume, which is given by</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>7</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi>R</mi></math></p>"
  }
